@page "/logs"

<CatHead Title="Logs">
    A line-of-business app's own log, read in place: a deploy being watched, the entries behind a
    failed order, an audit trail. It is the <a href="/live-state"><code>.output</code></a> pane
    from Live states, with columns, highlighting, rows that expand and rows that act. The app
    filters, searches and pages the lines; the pane shows the ones it renders.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.LiveTail" Title="Live tail" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A <code>data-follow</code> pane sticks to the newest line and lets go when the reader scrolls
        up. Scroll this one up: Sedna.UI.js marks the pane <code>data-follow-paused</code> and the
        <code>.output-jump</code> button appears over its foot; <code>data-output-follow</code> on
        the button follows again. <code>.output-panel</code> holds the head, the pane and the
        button. <strong>Pausing is the app's</strong>: render the pane without
        <code>data-follow</code> and it stops following, render it with the attribute again and it
        jumps to the newest line. The pane also fires a bubbling <code>sedna-follow</code> event
        with <code>detail.following</code> — <code>@@onsedna-follow</code> with a
        <code>SednaFollowEventArgs</code> in Blazor — for an app that counts the lines not yet seen.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.ApplicationLog" Title="Application log" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.output--columns</code> lays each line out as <code>.output-time</code>,
        <code>.output-level</code>, <code>.output-source</code> and <code>.output-msg</code>, aligned
        down the pane. The level and source columns are <code>--output-level-width</code> and
        <code>--output-source-width</code> on the pane. <code>.output-line--debug</code> and
        <code>--info</code> join <code>--warn</code> and <code>--error</code>: info colours only
        its level, because a pane of tinted info lines hides the warnings. The level icon is Remix
        Icon, and <strong>the word stays beside it</strong>. <code>.output-id</code>,
        <code>.output-num</code>, <code>.output-str</code> and <code>.output-key</code> highlight
        what the app marked up; the library never parses a message. On a warn or error line they
        take the line's colour.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.Plain" Title="Without highlighting" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The same columns with no level modifier and no token classes, for a log where colour would
        claim more than the data does &mdash; an audit trail, where every entry matters equally.
        Everything on this page is opt-in per line and per token.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.ExpandingRows" Title="Expanding rows" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A row with a stack trace, a request or the entry's fields is a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>
        with <code>.output-details</code> inside the line: the columns go in its
        <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code> and the rest in <code>.output-trace</code>. The caret sits in the
        row gutter, so expanding and plain rows line up. Opening a row in a following pane lets go of
        the tail when the row grows past the bottom, so the trace is not scrolled away. The pane
        stops at 320px and scrolls; <code>--output-max-height</code> on it sets another height, here
        32rem for a pane of rows that open.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.RowActions" Title="Row actions" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.output-actions</code> is a strip at the end of the line that shows on hover and on
        focus, and stays while its <a href="/menu"><code>.menu</code></a> is open. The buttons are
        <code>.btn--ghost .btn--icon .btn--sm</code>, the copy button is <code>data-copy</code>, and
        the menu is the usual <code>.menu-anchor</code> with <code>data-menu-toggle</code>. Put the
        strip <strong>after</strong> the <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, never inside its
        <code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>. On a touch screen the strip is always shown and the line keeps
        room for two buttons, so keep it to a copy button and a menu. An open menu holds a following pane still.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.Filters" Title="Level filters and wrapping" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The level filter is a set of <a href="/chip"><code>.chip</code></a> buttons in a
        <a href="/toolbar"><code>.toolbar</code></a>: <code>aria-pressed</code> for the state and
        <code>.chip--active</code> for the look, set together by the app, which then renders only
        the matching lines. Wrapping is a <a href="/segmented"><code>.segmented</code></a> setting
        that switches <code>.output--nowrap</code> on the pane.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.TimeRange" Title="A time range and auto-refresh" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; open it.</strong> The span of the log is a
        <code>.form-trigger</code> opening a <code>.popover</code> of
        <a href="/chip"><code>.chip</code></a> ranges and the reader's recent ones; the refresh
        interval is a small <a href="/form-select"><code>.form-select</code></a> beside a refresh
        button. The app fetches the lines for the range on its own timer. With a start and end of
        the reader's choosing, a quick select and an interval it can start and stop, it is the
        recipe on <a href="/form-datetime">Date and time</a>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.OneLineDense" Title="One line per entry, dense" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.output--nowrap</code> keeps every entry on one line and cuts the message with an
        ellipsis; the pane never scrolls sideways, so the row actions stay in view. Wrapping stays the
        default because a cut line hides the part of a message that differs &mdash; offer an expanding
        row for the whole entry. <code>.output--dense</code> tightens the rows for scanning a long
        stretch.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.Empty" Title="Nothing to show" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.output-empty</code> is an <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> in the pane holding an
        <a href="/empty-state"><code>.empty-state</code></a>, so the pane keeps its frame. Say
        whether the log is empty or the filter emptied it, and offer the way back.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.Loading" Title="Loading" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The first load is <a href="/skeleton"><code>.skeleton-text</code></a> lines. Fetching more
        around lines already shown is <code>.output-busy</code>, an <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> with a
        <code>.spinner</code> and a few words, at the end it is loading into. Mark the pane
        <code>aria-busy="true"</code> for both.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<h2 id="developer-output">Developer output <CatAnchor Id="developer-output" /></h2>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.Terminal" Title="Terminal" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.output--terminal</code> is the pane as a shell shows it: each command an
        <code>.output-line--prompt</code> holding <code>.output-prompt</code> &mdash; the app's
        <code>$</code>, <code>&gt;</code> or <code>PS&gt;</code>, hidden from a screen reader and from a
        selection &mdash; and <code>.output-cmd</code>. A command that wraps carries on under its own
        first character, not under the prompt, and a command after earlier output stands apart from it.
        Lines are not tinted on hover. Each command's copy button is the row's
        <code>.output-actions</code>; the whole session is a <code>data-copy-target</code> in the head.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.StackTrace" Title="Stack trace" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        An <code>.output-trace</code> read frame by frame: <code>.output-exception</code> says what was
        thrown, <code>.output-frames</code> lists the <code>.output-frame</code>s, each the call and its
        <code>.output-frame-loc</code> &mdash; a link where the app can open the file at that line, a
        <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> where it cannot. The location stands at the frame's end and drops under
        the call when the row is narrow. <code>.output-frame--external</code> is a frame in code the reader
        does not own, drawn quieter, and a run of them folds into a closed
        <code>details.output-frames-fold</code> so the frames that matter stand together.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.TestResults" Title="Test results" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        The run as a whole is a <a href="/bars"><code>.stack-bar</code></a> of <code>series-go</code>,
        <code>series-danger</code> and <code>series-muted</code> with its legend in words. Each suite is a
        <code>details.test-suite</code>, open where something failed, its summary the
        <code>.test-suite-name</code>, the <code>.test-suite-count</code> and the
        <code>.test-time</code>. Each test is a <code>.test-case</code> in <code>.test-cases</code>, under
        its suite's icon. <code>--pass</code>, <code>--fail</code> and <code>--skip</code> colour the
        icon, and <strong>the outcome is in words as well</strong>: the count on a suite, a
        <code>.visually-hidden</code> word on a test. A failed test's message follows it in an
        <code>.output-trace</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Logs.EnvironmentVariables" Title="Environment variables" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <strong>Live &mdash; show one.</strong> <a href="/card"><code>.kv</code></a> rows with a
        <code>.kv-action</code> column. <code>.secret-value</code> draws a value as dots, not selectable,
        until a <code>data-reveal</code> button whose <code>aria-controls</code> names it is pressed:
        Sedna.UI.js marks the value <code>data-sedna-revealed</code> and the button
        <code>aria-pressed</code>, and the button's two icons swap. Copy is <code>data-copy</code> per
        value and works masked or not. The value is in the page either way; one the page must not hold
        until the reader asks is the app's to fetch on the reveal.
    </p>
</CatExample>
